 ## Background
-Originating from the need to handle noise and ambiguity in neural implicit representations, uncertainty-aware methods were first explored by Saklani et al. [121] using Deep Ensembles and MC Dropout [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent work introduced single-model approaches like REV-INR, which uses a Normal Inverse-Gamma distribution to estimate both aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty in a single forward pass [S1, unofficial]. Probabilistic spatial super-resolution methods like PSRFlow extended this to normalizing flows [S1, unofficial]. These methods are primarily used in scientific volume visualization and active NeRF view selection.
+The integration of uncertainty quantification into neural rendering addresses the limitations of deterministic point estimates, which often fail to communicate confidence in noisy or sparse data regimes. Early work by Saklani et al. explored Deep Ensembles and Monte Carlo Dropout for INR volume visualization, establishing that ensembles correlate better with reconstruction error but at high storage costs [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent methods like REV-INR introduced single-model approaches using Normal Inverse-Gamma distributions to estimate both epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty in a single forward pass [S1, unofficial]. The trend has moved towards compact models with built-in uncertainty, such as PSRFlow, which uses normalizing flows for super-resolution [S1, unofficial].
 
